package RNG::HMAC_DRBG;

use v5.24;
use strict;
use warnings;
use RNG ();
use RNG::Provider ();
use RNG::Seed;

our $VERSION = '0.02';
our @ISA = 'RNG::Provider';

RNG::_load_xs();

sub seed_from_entropy {
    my ($class, @input) = @_;

    return RNG::Seed->from_bytes($class->_seed_from_entropy(@input));
}

sub new_from_entropy {
    my ($class, @input) = @_;

    return $class->new($class->seed_from_entropy(@input));
}

sub rand_U01_callback {
    my ($self) = @_;
    return sub { $self->rand_U01 };
}

sub srand {
    my ($self, @seed) = @_;
    my $automatic = !@seed || !defined $seed[0];
    my $value = $automatic ? $self->_srand : $seed[0];

    $self->_srand($value) unless $automatic;

    return $self->is_secure
        ? RNG::Seed->redacted(ref $self)
        : RNG::Seed->from_bytes($value)
        if $automatic;
    return $seed[0] if $seed[0];
    return RNG::Seed->from_string($seed[0]);
}

1;

=head1 NAME

RNG::HMAC_DRBG - an HMAC-SHA-256 deterministic random bit generator

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    use RNG::HMAC_DRBG;

    # Deterministic mode, useful for tests and reproducible simulations.
    my $test_rng = RNG::HMAC_DRBG->new(42);

    # Secure mode, initialized from operating-system entropy.
    my $rng = RNG::HMAC_DRBG->new_secure('application name');

    {
        local ${^RNG} = $rng;
        print rand(10), "\n";
    }

=head1 DESCRIPTION

This module implements the HMAC_DRBG mechanism using HMAC-SHA-256.  The
construction follows the HMAC_DRBG mechanism specified by NIST SP 800-90A,
but this module is not a FIPS-validated cryptographic module.

The provider has two deliberately distinct construction modes.  C<new> takes
a caller-provided seed and creates a deterministic generator.  C<new_secure>
obtains entropy from the operating system and creates a generator which can
reseed itself, detect a process fork, and optionally use prediction
resistance.  Do not use a caller-provided predictable seed when security is
required.  On native Win32 builds the compatibility layer uses the Windows
C<RtlGenRandom> API, exported as C<SystemFunction036>.  On Unix-like builds
it uses C<getentropy> when available, otherwise C</dev/urandom>.

The state is stored in a blessed scalar reference and the implementation is
in XS.  The module provides C<get_rand_U01_XS_func_addr> and
C<get_rand_U01_XS_state_addr>, allowing the core's
C<rand> path to obtain U01 values without Perl method dispatch or a temporary byte
buffer.

=head1 SECURITY NOTES

This is intended to be a serious cryptographic provider, but using it does not
by itself make an application secure.  Secure use still requires a suitable
operating-system entropy source, correct key and state handling, appropriate
reseed policy, and an application that uses random values for the right
purpose.  Formal conformance or validation is outside the scope of this
module.

Calling C<srand> with an explicit seed switches the object to deterministic
mode.  A false explicit seed string is returned as a true C<RNG::Seed> object
which stringifies to that seed and uses the same string expansion when
replayed.  Calling C<srand> without a seed on a secure object obtains fresh
operating-system entropy instead.  On a platform where obtaining sufficient
operating-system entropy can block, secure construction, reseeding, and this
form of C<srand> can block too.

=head1 METHODS

See L<RNG> for the common provider interface used by C<${^RNG}>.

=head2 new( SEED )

Create a deterministic HMAC_DRBG from SEED.  This mode is intended for tests
and reproducible simulations, not for generating secrets.

=head2 seed_from_entropy( ENTROPY, [NONCE, [PERSONALIZATION]] )

Apply the separate instantiation inputs defined by NIST SP 800-90A and return
a C<RNG::Seed> which contains the resulting 64-octet C<Key || V> state. The
inputs are passed directly to the construction; they must be octet strings,
rather than ordinary Perl seed strings. This is useful for interoperability,
checking published HMAC_DRBG vectors, and replaying the resulting state.

The caller is responsible for supplying input with sufficient entropy for its
required security strength.

=head2 new_from_entropy( ENTROPY, [NONCE, [PERSONALIZATION]] )

Equivalent to C<< $class->new($class->seed_from_entropy(...)) >>. It creates
a deterministic provider and does not enable automatic secure reseeding. Use
C<new_secure> for operating-system entropy and secure-mode reseeding.

=head2 new_secure( [PERSONALIZATION] )

Create a secure-mode provider using operating-system entropy.  An optional
personalization string is incorporated during instantiation.  The constructor
fails if a secure operating-system entropy source is unavailable.

=head2 rand_bytes( LENGTH )

Return exactly LENGTH generated bytes.  Secure-mode providers automatically
reseed after their configured interval and after a detected process fork.

=head2 rand( [LIMIT] )

Return a floating-point value between zero and one, or between zero and LIMIT
when LIMIT is supplied.  A LIMIT of zero is treated as one.

=head2 rand_U01

Return a U01 value, which is a uniform floating-point value in the half-open
interval [0,1).

=head2 rand_U01_callback

Return a callback which calls C<rand_U01> on this provider.

=head2 srand( [SEED] )

Reset the provider.  An explicit SEED selects deterministic mode.  With no
seed, a secure-mode provider obtains fresh operating-system entropy and
returns a redacted C<RNG::Seed>.  A deterministic provider obtains fresh seed
material and returns a replayable C<RNG::Seed>.  Use C<reseed> when
deterministic additional input is wanted.

=head2 reseed( [ADDITIONAL_INPUT] )

Reseed a secure provider from operating-system entropy, optionally combined
with additional input.  A deterministic provider must supply additional input
and uses it as deterministic reseed material.

=head2 reseed_interval( [COUNT] )

Read or set the maximum number of generate operations between automatic
reseeds.  The default is one million operations.

=head2 prediction_resistance( [BOOLEAN] )

Read or set prediction resistance.  Enabling it requires a secure-mode
provider and causes fresh operating-system entropy to be obtained before each
generation request.

=head2 get_rand_U01_XS_func_addr and get_rand_U01_XS_state_addr

Return the callback address and the address of its state used by the core's
fast C<rand> path.  Applications should not normally call these methods
directly.

=head1 SEE ALSO

L<RNG>, L<perlfunc/rand EXPR>, and
L<https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/90/a/r1/final>.

=cut
